---
/**
 * Signed month-1 deliverable, and the thing that makes analytics lawful on
 * this site. Two facts still outstanding before this is final:
 *   - the ICO registration number, once registration completes
 *   - the retention period, once the client confirms how long he keeps enquiries
 *
 * Ported to the design system 2 Sep 2026. The wording is unchanged.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Wrap from "../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import Display from "../components/ds/Display.astro";
import Kicker from "../components/ds/Kicker.astro";
import SectionHead from "../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import MonoLabel from "../components/ds/MonoLabel.astro";
import Link from "../components/ds/Link.astro";
import { BUSINESS } from "../data/site";

/* September, not August (5 Sep 2026). The notice was revised substantively
   twice in the week before launch: 52f57d9 rewrote the analytics section for
   the consent gate, and 5fb925d changed who it discloses can see enquiry data,
   which is a UK GDPR Article 13(1)(e) disclosure. A revision date is itself a
   factual claim and this one was checkable against the file's own history.
   Bump it with any edit to this page, as cookies.astro already does. */
const updated = "November 2026";
---

<Base
  title="Privacy notice"
  description="How Structure & Style handles information you send through this site: what is collected, why, who processes it, how long it is kept and your rights."
>
  <Wrap style="padding-top:var(--hero-y);padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <Kicker scribe>Privacy</Kicker>
    <Display level={2} as="h1" style="margin:var(--space-12) 0 var(--space-10)">
      Privacy notice
    </Display>
    <MonoLabel as="p">Last updated {updated}</MonoLabel>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style="padding-bottom:var(--section-y)">
    <div style="max-width:var(--measure);display:grid;gap:var(--space-15)">
      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>Who we are</SectionHead>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          This site belongs to {BUSINESS.legalName}, registered in England and Wales, company number{" "}
          {BUSINESS.companyNumber}. We are the data controller for the information described here.
          You can reach us at <Link href={`mailto:${BUSINESS.email}`}>{BUSINESS.email}</Link>{" "}
          or {BUSINESS.phoneDisplay}.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>What we collect, and why</SectionHead>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          If you send an enquiry we collect your name, phone number, email address, the area you are
          in, whatever you tell us about the job, any photographs or documents you attach, and how you heard about
          us. We use it to answer your enquiry, quote for the work, and carry it out if you go ahead.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          The lawful basis is taking steps at your request before entering a contract, and our
          legitimate interest in responding to people who contact us.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>Files you send us</SectionHead>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          Photographs, plans and documents you attach to an enquiry help us understand the space and
          price the work. We store them privately and include access links in the enquiry record for
          the people handling it. We do not publish them. Photographs of completed work are only
          used in our own marketing where the customer has agreed to it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>Analytics</SectionHead>
        {/* Rewritten 4 Sep 2026, when analytics went behind consent. The old
            wording described what Google Analytics does and said nothing about
            when it runs, which was on every page from first paint. */}
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          We use Google Analytics to understand how people find and use this site: pages viewed,
          roughly where visitors are, what device they use, and which links they tap. IP addresses
          are shortened by Google before storage and we cannot identify you from any of it. We do not
          use analytics for advertising.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          It runs only if you accept it. The notice at the bottom of the page asks once, analytics
          stays off until you say yes, and ignoring the notice leaves it off. The lawful basis is
          your consent, and you can withdraw it at any time on our{" "}
          <Link href="/cookies/">cookies page</Link>, which lists exactly what is stored.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>Who else sees your information</SectionHead>
        {/* The named Flow to Form sentence came out on 5 Sep 2026, on Denis's
            instruction. It read: "Flowtoform Limited, trading as Flow to Form,
            maintains this website for us and may see enquiry data in the course
            of that work, under a written agreement."

            Two reasons it was right to go, beyond the instruction. It named the
            agency on the client's own privacy notice, which is the client's
            document and not a place for a supplier credit. And it asserted a
            written agreement that does not exist: the DPA is still unwritten,
            so on launch day that clause would have been untrue.

            WHAT THIS COSTS, stated so nobody has to rediscover it. UK GDPR
            Article 13(1)(e) requires the recipients or categories of recipients
            of personal data. Netlify and Google are still named below, so the
            notice is not silent, but the agency that can see enquiry data is no
            longer disclosed at all. The compliant middle is one nameless
            clause, "the agency that maintains this website for us can see
            enquiry data in the course of that work", and it should go back in
            the day the DPA is signed, when it will also be true.

            Back in, nameless, Nov 2026 with the trade emails. Merge this only
            once the data agreement is signed. */}
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          Our website is hosted by Netlify, and enquiries submitted through the form are processed by
          them on our behalf. Cloudflare stores files attached to enquiries and checks file uploads
          for automated misuse. Analytics data is processed by Google. If you choose to message us on
          WhatsApp, that conversation runs through WhatsApp, which is operated by Meta under its own
          terms and privacy policy. The agency that maintains this website for us can see enquiry
          data in the course of that work, under a written agreement. We do not sell your information
          to anyone and we do not add people who send us an enquiry to marketing lists.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>How long we keep it</SectionHead>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          Enquiries that do not become jobs are kept while there is a realistic prospect of the work
          going ahead, then deleted. Records relating to completed work are kept for six years, which
          is the period we need them for tax and for any claim about the work.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>If we email your business</SectionHead>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          We sometimes email limited companies and LLPs whose work suits ours, such as builders,
          interior designers, architects and property agents, to ask if they need fitted joinery. We
          do not email sole traders or ordinary partnerships this way, and we send at most three
          emails.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          We hold the contact's name and work email address, the company's name and details, the
          project on its website that we mentioned, and anything we write to each other. We take these
          from the company's own website and from Companies House. We do not buy lists.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          The lawful basis is our legitimate interest in finding work for our business. We have
          weighed that against your rights and keep a written record of it.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          Some of these emails include a link to a page showing our work. That page records each time
          it is opened: the time, whether it was a phone, computer or tablet, the rough place and the
          network's name. It stores no cookie on your device and no IP address, and each record is
          deleted after 180 days. We use it only to decide whether to follow up by email.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          The agency that helps us with this handles these details for us under a written agreement,
          using its own tools, which include an AI assistant. So do our email provider, Google, and
          the company that hosts the page, Cloudflare.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          If you do not reply, we delete your details 6 months after our last email. You can object at
          any time, with no need to give a reason: reply "stop" to any email, or write to{" "}
          <Link href={`mailto:${BUSINESS.email}`}>{BUSINESS.email}</Link>. We then stop at once and keep
          only your address on a do-not-contact list, so that we never write to you again.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <SectionHead level={5}>Your rights</SectionHead>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          You can ask us for a copy of the information we hold about you, ask us to correct it, ask
          us to delete it, or object to how we are using it. Email{" "}
          <Link href={`mailto:${BUSINESS.email}`}>{BUSINESS.email}</Link> and we will
          reply within a month.
        </p>
        <p style="margin-top:var(--space-8)">
          If you are unhappy with how we have handled your information you can complain to us first at{" "}
          <Link href={`mailto:${BUSINESS.email}`}>{BUSINESS.email}</Link>, and we will acknowledge your
          complaint within 30 days. You can also complain to the UK data protection regulator, the
          Information Commission (ICO), at{" "}
          <Link href="https://ico.org.uk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ico.org.uk</Link>.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</Base>
